    "summary": "\n<p><strong>The theorem.</strong> If a <em>sparse</em> language is NP-hard under polynomial-time many-one reductions, then \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\). Proved over complexitylib's multi-tape Turing machines, with \\(\\mathrm P\\), \\(\\mathrm{NP}\\), \\(\\mathrm{FP}\\), \\(\\le_p\\), \\(\\mathrm{NPHard}\\) and Cook–Levin all complexitylib's. The proof is the Ogihara–Watanabe pruning argument made polynomial-time on a machine: the loop is in FP (<code>iterS_mem_FP</code>) and decides SAT.</p>\n<p><strong>Scope.</strong> A separate Lake project (Lean 4.35.0-rc3), built apart from the main library (about 2,300 complexitylib modules, roughly 20 minutes on one Mac). Its headline declarations carry an audit file (<code>Mahaney/Audit.lean</code>); the project's <code>PLAN.md</code> records “propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound only”.</p><div class=\"ldeps\"><div><span class=\"ldeps-k\">Rests on</span><em>nothing in this figure</em></div><div><span class=\"ldeps-k\">Feeds</span><a class=\"lchip\" href=\"#cxbarrier-TableBarrier\">Barrier for the table problem<span class=\"lchip-via\">pruning argument</span></a><a class=\"lchip\" href=\"#cxbarrier-PolicyBarrier\">Barrier for a policy language</a></div></div>",
