    "summary": "\n<p><strong>The problem.</strong> An instance is \\((\\text{mode},k,K)\\); it is a yes-instance when some contract-valid schedule scores at most \\(K\\), where the <em>policy chooses its own points</em> (<code>ValidPointList mode k pts</code>) and the score is the structural \\(S=A+5L\\). Encoding: the mode as one bit, \\(k\\) in <strong>unary</strong>, \\(K\\) in canonical binary; malformed strings are no-instances.</p>\n<p><strong>The theorem.</strong> If this language is NP-hard under polynomial-time many-one reductions, then \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\). It has no hypotheses. The reason is a bound proved in Lean: the optimum is at most \\(B(k)=15k(2k-1)\\) (<code>OPT_le_B</code>, from a uniform feasible schedule), so any budget above that is a trivial yes; <code>reduceS</code> in FP maps the language to a core with budgets of at most \\(\\mathrm{size}(B(k))\\) bits, and the core is sparse (<code>CoreL_sparse</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>What it is not.</strong> It is not a hardness result and does not put the problem in P. It says nothing about a Turing or other reduction notion, or about the problem with the KMY points fixed (no Lean statement for that problem).</p><div class=\"ldeps\"><div><span class=\"ldeps-k\">Rests on</span><a class=\"lchip\" href=\"#cxbarrier-Mahaney\">Mahaney's theorem<span class=\"lchip-via\">pruning argument</span></a></div><div><span class=\"ldeps-k\">Feeds</span><a class=\"lchip\" href=\"#cxbarrier-PolicyBarrier\">Barrier for a policy language<span class=\"lchip-via\">abstracted</span></a><a class=\"lchip\" href=\"#cxbarrier-OpenPosed\">Open: NP-hardness of the table problem<span class=\"lchip-via\">if NP-hard then P = NP</span></a></div></div>",
