    "summary": "\n<p><strong>The theorem.</strong> <code>GenLang</code>, the table problem with the points supplied as input, is NP-hard under polynomial-time many-one reductions, already at arity 2: SAT ≤ₚ GenLang. The instance is encoded as \\(k\\) in unary, the budget in binary, the points as blocks.</p>\n<p><strong>Scope, exactly.</strong> It is for the structural score \\(S=A+5L\\) of the benchmark. It is <strong>not</strong> a T-count statement, <strong>not</strong> for the KMY points, and <strong>not</strong> for the problem in which the policy chooses its own points (which the barrier makes NP-hardness of unlikely). What it shows is that the ordering of the points alone carries combinatorial difficulty.</p><div class=\"ldeps\"><div><span class=\"ldeps-k\">Rests on</span><a class=\"lchip\" href=\"#cxchain-SatDhc\">SAT → DHC₂<span class=\"lchip-via\">satRed_correct</span></a><a class=\"lchip\" href=\"#cxchain-PolyTime\">The reduction is polynomial-time<span class=\"lchip-via\">satRed_mem_FP</span></a><a class=\"lchip\" href=\"#cxchain-CookLevin\">SAT is NP-hard<span class=\"lchip-via\">SAT is NP-hard</span></a></div><div><span class=\"ldeps-k\">Feeds</span><a class=\"lchip\" href=\"#cxchain-OpenBridge\">Open: from supplied points to the real problem<span class=\"lchip-via\">points are supplied</span></a></div></div>",
